Hello, you may remember me from year ago, as a great defender of 
abuse. Here's my opinion (probably shared by none):

Setting the computer's clock to get +1 Luck is Pernicious Cheating, 
even in my highly-permissive opinion. 

Rerolling for more interesting (or better) equipment is not 
cheating, it's just a boring and pointless way to spend your 
time. 

Making a screen-scraping robot that starts the game, sees that 
there's a wand on the floor in room 1, gets it, Engraves, wishes, 
and saves (and otherwise quits and restarts) is certain to get you 
flamed here. It will also fill up your nethack logfile.

Quitting when you absolutely dislike your current situation is a
reasonable strategy for maximising your enjoyment per time spent. 
It may not be the best way to maximize learning per time spent.

Playing for an early jackpot is neither cheating nor abuse. I used
to ascend more often when I did that.

Most abuses are boring. In fact, any action which is repeated turn
after turn after turn for small gain is probably an abuse. This 
includes boulder-pushing, pudding-farming, and the protection 
racket. I believe people should be free to use any abuse they 
wish, as much as they want, without being looked down on here. 

Mind you, there are degrees of abuse; engraving on every square 
and dropping a rock is a rather grungy game plan (but I once 
cleverly did it for a short while in order to save a character 
who had run into difficulties but who had plenty of food), but
the protection racket, though it may be an abuse, is difficult 
to do and can sometimes become extremely non-boring.

